source":"gtwo_kml_202610201152","idp_filedate":1792497125000,"nhcTitle":"Off the East Coast of the United States (AL93)","discussion":"A storm-force non-tropical low pressure area located a few hundred  miles off the North Carolina coast is producing some shower and  thunderstorm activity primarily west of its center. However,  overnight satellite wind data indicated the low was still attached  to an occluded front. Over the next couple of days, the low could  develop some organized deep convection and gradually lose its  frontal features while it moves slowly north-northeastward over the  Gulf Stream, allowing the system to briefly develop subtropical  characteristics. By the end of the week, the system is expected to  accelerate northeastward away from the the eastern United States,  where environmental conditions are expected to become unfavorable  for additional subtropical or tropical development.  1. Regardless of the status of the system, high winds, coastal  flooding, and dangerous surf are expected along portions of the U.S.  East Coast during the next few days. For more information, please  consult statements and warnings from local National Weather Service  offices and the Weather Prediction Center, along with High Seas  Forecasts and Storm Warnings issued by the National Weather Service. \n Formation chance through 48 hours...medium...40 percent. \n Formation chance through 7 days...medium...40 percent.","disturbance":1}},{"type":"Feature","geometry":{"type":"Polygon","coordinates":[[[-94.24984137640128,9.341612452062483],[-94.21304340325352,9.38722270938454],[-94.17807613601572,9.434293185060064],[-94.1449165290677,9.482722943554053],[-94.11354153678924,9.532411049331504],[-94.08392811356016,9.583256566857411],[-94.05605321376024,9.635158560596777],[-94.0298937917693,9.688016095014595],[-94.0054268019671,9.741728234575863],[-93.98262919873346,9.796194043745578],[-93.9614779364482,9.851312586988737],[-93.94194996949109,9.906982928770336],[-93.92402225224